# Line endings are a property of the repository, not of the machine that cloned it.
#
# `.editorconfig` mandates `end_of_line = lf`, and both required CI gates check it:
# `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` (the first step of the .NET job) and `pnpm format:check`
# (the first step of the PWA job, Prettier with `"endOfLine": "lf"`). Without this file a clone made
# with Git's Windows default of `core.autocrlf=true` produces a CRLF working tree, which fails both
# gates on every file and makes `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild` report IDE0055 across the solution — a
# breakage that looks like a code defect and is not one.
#
# `docs/dev/setup.md` section 2 asks each developer to set `core.autocrlf=false` before cloning.
# That instruction stays good advice, but nothing enforced it; this file does, for everyone,
# including a clone made before they read the setup guide.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the working tree, detecting text versus binary automatically.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even if a future rule above is relaxed: `scripts/dev` runs under bash in
# CI and in the devcontainer, and a CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter unfindable.
*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/dev text eol=lf

# Windows-only entry points are the one place CRLF is correct: cmd and PowerShell read them natively.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary assets Git must never translate or diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.otf binary
